{"name":"Martian Haze","hex":"#adeace","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"martian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Martian (“of or pertaining to the planet Mars”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Martian packet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/martian"},{"term":"haze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Very fine solid particles (smoke, dust) or liquid droplets (moisture) suspended in the air, slightly limiting visibility.","A reduction of transparency of a clear gas or liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be or become hazy, or thick with haze."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/haze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mars in fiction","blocks":[{"text":"Mars, the fourth planet from the Sun, has appeared as a setting in works of fiction since at least the mid-1600s. Trends in the planet's portrayal have largely been influenced by advances in planetary science. It became the most popular celestial object in fiction in the late 1800s, when it became clear that there was no life on the Moon. The predominant genre depicting Mars at the time was utopian fiction. Around the same time, the mistaken belief that there are canals on Mars emerged and made its way into fiction, popularized by Percival Lowell's speculations of an ancient civilization having constructed them. The War of the Worlds, H. G. Wells's novel about an alien invasion of Earth by sinister Martians, was published in 1897 and went on to have a major influence on the science fiction genre."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/The_War_of_the_Worlds_by_Henrique_Alvim_Corr%C3%AAa%2C_original_graphic_15.jpg/330px-The_War_of_the_Worlds_by_Henrique_Alvim_Corr%C3%AAa%2C_original_graphic_15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_in_fiction","wikidata":"Q2661218"},{"title":"Haze","blocks":[{"text":"Haze is traditionally an atmospheric phenomenon in which dust, smoke, and other dry particulates suspended in air obscure visibility and the clarity of the sky. The World Meteorological Organization manual of codes includes a classification of particulates causing horizontal obscuration into categories of fog, ice fog, steam fog, mist, haze, smoke, volcanic ash, dust, sand, and snow. Sources for particles that cause haze include farming, traffic, industry, windy weather, volcanic activity and wildfires.\nSeen from afar and depending on the direction of view with respect to the Sun, haze may appear brownish or bluish, while mist tends to be bluish grey instead. Whereas haze often is considered a phenomenon occurring in dry air, mist formation is a phenomenon in saturated, humid air. However, haze particles may act as condensation nuclei that leads to the subsequent vapor condensation and formation of mist droplets; such forms of haze are known as \"wet haze\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/MosqueinAbuja.jpg/330px-MosqueinAbuja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haze","wikidata":"Q643546"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Solar_system.jpg/500px-Solar_system.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Solar_system.jpg/1280px-Solar_system.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Solar_system.jpg/500px-Solar_system.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early depictions of Mars in fiction were often part of tours of the Solar System. Clicking on a planet leads to the article about its depiction in fiction.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASolar_system.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/A_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg/500px-A_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/A_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg/1280px-A_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/A_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg/500px-A_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Plunge into Space, an 1890 piece of utopian fiction set on Mars","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Plunge_into_Space%2C_cover_image.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Orson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg/500px-Orson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Orson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg/1280px-Orson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Orson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg/500px-Orson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orson Welles interviewed by reporters after his 1938 radio adaptation of The War of the Worlds caused a panic","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrson_Welles_War_of_the_Worlds_1938.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Klaatu_-_screenshot_from_trailer_for_Day_the_Earth_Stood_Still.jpg/500px-Klaatu_-_screenshot_from_trailer_for_Day_the_Earth_Stood_Still.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Klaatu_-_screenshot_from_trailer_for_Day_the_Earth_Stood_Still.jpg/500px-Klaatu_-_screenshot_from_trailer_for_Day_the_Earth_Stood_Still.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Klaatu, the Martian who visits Earth in the 1951 film The Day the Earth Stood Still","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKlaatu_-_screenshot_from_trailer_for_Day_the_Earth_Stood_Still.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Planet_stories_195103.jpg/330px-Planet_stories_195103.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Planet_stories_195103.jpg/330px-Planet_stories_195103.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Decadent portrayals of Martians were popularized by Edgar Rice Burroughs, inspiring many authors such as Leigh Brackett. Seen here is the March 1951 cover of Planet Stories, featuring Brackett's \"Black Amazon of Mars\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlanet_stories_195103.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Mariner_4_craters.gif/500px-Mariner_4_craters.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Mariner_4_craters.gif/1280px-Mariner_4_craters.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Mariner_4_craters.gif/500px-Mariner_4_craters.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Data returned from Mars exploration missions in the 1960s and 1970s, such as this photograph by the Mariner 4 probe, led to stories of life on Mars becoming unfashionable.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMariner_4_craters.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedi